@meta
id: 16-12
title: Drill — side by side
chapter: 16
type: drill
skills: split-window, window-nav
par: 110
minutes: 6
reps: 8
vscode: open the second file, drag its tab to the right, edit, edit
open: customer.entity.ts
vimrc: set wildmenu wildmode=full

@brief
Eight times: open the customer repository beside the entity, give both
a `phone` field, write, quit. The repository is not in the list; you
open it. In the entity the field is `phone?: string;` under `email`,
and in the repository it is `phone: record.phone ?? undefined,` under
its `email` line.

Filenames complete on the command line too. `:vs customer.r` and Tab
finishes the name.

@keys
| `:vs customer.r` `Tab` | open it on the left |
| `Ctrl-W l` | back to the entity on the right |
| `:wqa` | write both, quit |

@hints
`:vs customer.r`, Tab, Enter. `/email`, `o`, the mapping, `Esc`.
`Ctrl-W l`. `/email`, `o`, the field, `Esc`. `:wqa`.
--
The drill is the shape: open beside, edit left, hop right, edit, write
all. Do not touch `:b` at all in this one.

@solution
    :vs customer.r<Tab><Enter>  /email<Enter>  o phone: record.phone ?? undefined, <Esc>
    Ctrl-W l  /email<Enter>  o phone?: string; <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

Under a hundred keystrokes with the Tab, and it would be a hundred and
ten without.

By the end of the reps `Ctrl-W l` should feel like `l`: the same
direction, one level up. That is the whole design of the `Ctrl-W`
family. Anything you can do to move the cursor between characters,
`Ctrl-W` does between windows.

@start customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
};

@expected customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  phone?: string;
  name: string;
};

@start customer.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Customer as CustomerRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Customer } from './customer.entity';

@injectable()
export class CustomerRepository extends BaseRepository<CustomerRecord, Customer> {
  public toEntity(record: CustomerRecord): Customer {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      email: record.email,
      name: record.name,
    };
  }
}

@expected customer.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Customer as CustomerRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Customer } from './customer.entity';

@injectable()
export class CustomerRepository extends BaseRepository<CustomerRecord, Customer> {
  public toEntity(record: CustomerRecord): Customer {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      email: record.email,
      phone: record.phone ?? undefined,
      name: record.name,
    };
  }
}
